    "title": "Freeze watch issued for 16 counties. Rain, much colder temperatures in N.J. forecast.",
    "text": "WeatherThe National Weather Service has issued a freeze watch for 16 counties in central and southern New Jersey from late Monday into Tuesday morning.Len Melisurgo | NJ.com | CanvaUPDATE: Freeze watch expands to nearly entire state with temps in 20s in Monday night forecastA freeze watch has been issued for most of New Jersey starting Monday night after rain and much colder temperatures sweep through the state on Sunday. The watch runs through Tuesday morning, with overnight low temperatures in the 20s expected, the National Weather Service said. A coastal flood advisory is also in effect Saturday night for the Jersey Shore, Delaware Bay and areas along the Delaware River. Minor tidal flooding is possible. Rain showers will move across the state tonight and into Sunday morning as a cold front passes through, with rainfall amounts up to a half-inch.Sunday’s high temperatures will occur early in the day and fall sharply behind the front. Some areas could see temperatures 20 degrees colder than Saturday. Wind gusts of up to 20 mph will make it feel even colder.High temperatures on Monday will only reach the 40s and 50s, which is several degrees below average for mid-April.The freeze watch on Monday night includes 16 of New Jersey’s 21 counties, excluding Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Passaic and Union. New Jersey’s growing season is underway statewide, making the freeze particularly concerning for plants and gardens.Tuesday will remain below average with highs in the 40s and 50s.Temperatures are expected to climb back above average by Wednesday and continue warming through Friday, with highs potentially reaching the 60s and lower 70s by Thursday and Friday.Current weather radarN.J. weather: Nice warmup on the way, followed by cool and rainy weekendN.J. weather: Clouds, rain in forecast for today. Early weekend forecast looks wet, chilly.N.J. weather: Temperatures to briefly hit 70s before cold, rainy weekend. Latest forecast.The Advance Local Express Desk uses AI tools to assist in producing content, which is reviewed and edited by staff. Advance Local is the parent company of AL.com, Cleveland.com, lehighvalleylive.com,...",
